            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon A\vdash B(x)\colon Type
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            of “dependently typed
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon A
            an element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(x)
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of the type theory in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            It includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            as the special case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is not dependent on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            as a special case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the type of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Booleans"} Booleans
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sequence+types"} dependent sequence types
            as a special case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
          %h2#overview Overview
          %div
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+function+types"} dependent function types
              (aka “dependent product types” or “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              \-types”):
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DependentFunctionTypeInference-230215.jpg", :width => "770px"}/
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p Like any type constructor in type theory, a dependent product type is specified by rules saying when we can introduce it as a type, how to construct terms of that type, how to use or “eliminate” terms of that type, and how to compute when we combine the constructors with the eliminators.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rule"} type formation rule
            for dependent product type is:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{A\colon Type \qquad x\colon A \vdash B(x) \colon Type}{\prod_{x\colon A} B(x)\colon Type}
          %h3#as_a_negative_type As a negative type
          %p
            Dependent product types are almost always defined as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+types"} negative types
            %em apply
            it to any input:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{f\colon \prod_{x\colon A} B(x) \qquad a\colon A}{f(a) \colon B(a)}
          %p
            The constructor is then determined as usual for a negative type: to construct a term of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(x)
            containing a free variable
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon A
            = succeed "-abstraction\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{x\colon A\vdash b\colon B(x)}{\lambda x.b\colon \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)}
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            rule is the obvious one, saying that when we evaluate a
            = succeed "-abstraction," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.b)(a) \;\to_\beta\; b[a/x]
          %p
            If we want an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"} eta-conversion
            rule, the natural one says that every dependently typed function is a
            = succeed "-abstraction:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi η
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.f(x) \;\to_\eta\; f
          %h3#as_a_positive_type As a positive type
          %p
            It is also possible to present dependent product types as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+type"} positive type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            = succeed "-abstraction)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi funsplit
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{(x\colon A \vdash b\colon B(x)) \vdash c\colon C \qquad f\colon \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)}{funsplit(c,f)\colon C}
          %p
            This rule cannot be formulated in the usual presentation of type theory, since it involves a “higher-order judgment”: the context of the term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\colon C
            involves a “term of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(x)
            containing a free variable
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to depend on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)
          %p
            The natural
            = succeed "-reduction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi funsplit
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} funsplit(c, \lambda x.g) \;\to_\beta c[g/b]
          %p
            and its
            = succeed "-conversion" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi funsplit
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi η
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} funsplit(c[\lambda x.b / g], f) \;\to_\eta\; c[f/g].
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon \prod_{x\colon A} B(x) \vdash c\colon C
            is a term containing a free variable of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.b
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            which depends on “a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\colon B(x)
            containing a free variable
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon \prod_{x\colon A} B(x)
            = succeed "-rule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %h3#positive_versus_negative Positive versus negative
          %p As usual, the positive and negative formulations are equivalent in a suitable sense. They have the same constructor, while we can formulate the eliminators in terms of each other:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi funsplit
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ,
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi funsplit
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo ,
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  f(a) &amp;\coloneqq funsplit(b[a/x], f)\\
                  funsplit(c, f) &amp;\coloneqq c[f(x)/b]
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p The conversion rules also correspond.
          %p
            In dependent type theory, this definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi funsplit
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} funsplit
            only gives us a properly typed dependent eliminator if the negative dependent product type satisfies
            = succeed "-conversion." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion#Propositional"} propositional eta-conversion
            then we can transport along that instead—and conversely, the dependent eliminator allows us to prove propositional
            = succeed "-conversion." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GarnerSDP"} (Garner)
          %h3#dependent_product_types_a_la_russell_and_a_la_tarski Dependent product types a la Russell and a la Tarski
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
